1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,444
All five Chai Corner outlets
need fresh milk every morning.

2
00:00:04,744 --> 00:00:06,619
Three dairies want the deal.

3
00:00:06,619 --> 00:00:08,302
Ravi must pick just one.

4
00:00:08,852 --> 00:00:10,664
Kabir says, take the cheapest.

5
00:00:10,964 --> 00:00:12,999
Asha says, take the best milk.

6
00:00:12,999 --> 00:00:14,932
Meena says, take the closest.

7
00:00:15,232 --> 00:00:18,045
Everyone has a reason, and nobody agrees.

8
00:00:18,695 --> 00:00:21,386
No guessing, and no loudest voice.

9
00:00:21,386 --> 00:00:23,105
Ravi uses pure logic.

10
00:00:23,805 --> 00:00:27,636
Ravi sits down at the head office
with every fact he can find.

11
00:00:27,986 --> 00:00:31,434
Prices, delivery records, and
a taste test of each milk.

12
00:00:31,784 --> 00:00:34,819
This time, he will score
every option, one by one.

13
00:00:35,469 --> 00:00:37,859
So, what is rational decision making?

14
00:00:38,159 --> 00:00:42,895
Rational decision making means using
logic to choose the option that gives

15
00:00:42,895 --> 00:00:44,145
the most value.

16
00:00:44,645 --> 00:00:47,186
It pictures a perfect decision maker.

17
00:00:47,186 --> 00:00:50,089
Economists call this person, economic man.

18
00:00:50,439 --> 00:00:54,641
Fully logical, with all the facts,
who knows every option and where each

19
00:00:54,641 --> 00:00:55,541
one leads.

20
00:00:55,781 --> 00:00:59,210
And it always picks the very
best, not just a good one.

21
00:00:59,210 --> 00:01:00,817
That is called optimising.

22
00:01:01,467 --> 00:01:03,862
The rational model follows six steps.

23
00:01:04,262 --> 00:01:05,162
One.

24
00:01:04,819 --> 00:01:06,324
Define the problem.

25
00:01:06,324 --> 00:01:08,952
Five outlets need one milk supplier.

26
00:01:09,302 --> 00:01:10,202
Two.

27
00:01:09,799 --> 00:01:14,379
Pick the criteria, the things that
matter: quality, reliability, price,

28
00:01:14,379 --> 00:01:15,373
and distance.

29
00:01:15,723 --> 00:01:16,623
Three.

30
00:01:16,340 --> 00:01:17,320
Weight them.

31
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:20,018
Quality matters most, so it gets four.

32
00:01:20,018 --> 00:01:23,010
Reliability three,
price two, distance one.

33
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:24,260
Four.

34
00:01:23,878 --> 00:01:27,810
List the alternatives: Green
Farm, Hill Farm, and River Dairy.

35
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:29,060
Five.

36
00:01:28,731 --> 00:01:32,193
Rate each one on each
criterion, from one to ten.

37
00:01:32,543 --> 00:01:34,177
Green Farm is cheapest.

38
00:01:34,177 --> 00:01:36,179
Hill Farm has the best milk.

39
00:01:36,179 --> 00:01:38,998
River Dairy is never late, and close by.

40
00:01:39,348 --> 00:01:40,248
Six.

41
00:01:39,858 --> 00:01:41,237
Compute and choose.

42
00:01:41,237 --> 00:01:44,997
Multiply each rating by its
weight, then add it all up.

43
00:01:45,347 --> 00:01:47,135
Green Farm scores sixty.

44
00:01:47,135 --> 00:01:48,976
Hill Farm, sixty seven.

45
00:01:48,976 --> 00:01:51,198
And River Dairy, seventy six.

46
00:01:51,548 --> 00:01:52,781
River Dairy wins.

47
00:01:52,781 --> 00:01:56,991
Not the cheapest, not the best
milk, but the most value overall.

48
00:01:57,641 --> 00:02:01,066
But the rational model rests
on four big assumptions.

49
00:02:01,416 --> 00:02:02,316
One.

50
00:02:01,952 --> 00:02:03,569
The problem is clear.

51
00:02:03,869 --> 00:02:04,769
Two.

52
00:02:04,418 --> 00:02:06,130
Every option is known.

53
00:02:06,430 --> 00:02:07,330
Three.

54
00:02:06,972 --> 00:02:09,905
What you want is clear,
and it does not change.

55
00:02:10,205 --> 00:02:11,105
And four.

56
00:02:10,954 --> 00:02:13,310
There are no limits on time or money.

57
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:16,200
So where is this used at work today?

58
00:02:16,550 --> 00:02:17,657
Clear criteria.

59
00:02:17,657 --> 00:02:22,573
A hospital buying a scanner lists what
matters: safety, cost, and service.

60
00:02:22,923 --> 00:02:23,823
Weights.

61
00:02:23,663 --> 00:02:28,114
A team hiring a manager decides
that skills count more than years.

62
00:02:28,464 --> 00:02:29,701
All the options.

63
00:02:29,701 --> 00:02:33,423
A city building a bridge
invites bids from every builder.

64
00:02:33,773 --> 00:02:34,673
Scoring.

65
00:02:34,466 --> 00:02:38,794
Each bid gets points for price and
quality, so the choice is fair.

66
00:02:39,144 --> 00:02:40,075
Optimising.

67
00:02:40,075 --> 00:02:44,982
An airline uses software to find the
cheapest way to plan its flights.

68
00:02:45,632 --> 00:02:48,127
So is pure logic always the answer?

69
00:02:48,127 --> 00:02:50,622
The honest answer is, yes and no.

70
00:02:50,972 --> 00:02:52,071
The good side.

71
00:02:52,071 --> 00:02:54,872
It is clear, fair, and easy to explain.

72
00:02:54,872 --> 00:02:56,671
Anyone can check the sums.

73
00:02:57,021 --> 00:02:58,124
The hard side.

74
00:02:58,124 --> 00:03:00,731
Getting every fact takes time and money.

75
00:03:01,081 --> 00:03:03,675
And the four assumptions are rarely true.

76
00:03:03,675 --> 00:03:06,126
What about a fourth dairy nobody knew?

77
00:03:06,126 --> 00:03:07,995
Or a price jump next month?

78
00:03:08,345 --> 00:03:10,729
Even the weights are a judgment call.

79
00:03:10,729 --> 00:03:13,983
Make price count most, and
Green Farm wins instead.

80
00:03:14,333 --> 00:03:16,011
So the lesson is balance.

81
00:03:16,011 --> 00:03:18,360
Use the logic, but know its limits.

82
00:03:19,010 --> 00:03:19,926
Quick check.

83
00:03:19,926 --> 00:03:23,637
Ravi has listed quality,
reliability, price, and distance.

84
00:03:23,637 --> 00:03:25,195
What is the next step?

85
00:03:27,095 --> 00:03:28,441
Weight the criteria!

86
00:03:28,441 --> 00:03:32,261
Decide how much each one matters,
before you rate any option.

87
00:03:32,861 --> 00:03:33,871
So, remember.

88
00:03:34,171 --> 00:03:39,145
Rational decision making uses logic to
pick the option with the most value.

89
00:03:39,495 --> 00:03:44,046
Six steps: define, set criteria,
weight them, list options, rate them,

90
00:03:44,046 --> 00:03:44,964
and compute.

91
00:03:45,314 --> 00:03:49,185
But it assumes full facts and no
limits, and that is rarely true.

92
00:03:49,785 --> 00:03:52,001
Next time, bounded rationality.

93
00:03:52,001 --> 00:03:55,823
How real managers decide when
they cannot know everything.
